✅ What is JVM (Java Virtual Machine)?
The JVM is the engine that runs Java bytecode.
It’s part of the JRE and executes .class files produced by javac.

Bytecode = low-level, platform-independent instructions for the JVM
(NOT the same as CPU machine code).


🔹 What Are These Instructions?
.java --javac--> .class (bytecode)
JVM then either:
  - interprets bytecode, or
  - JIT-compiles hot code → native machine instructions at runtime

So: .class is never “raw CPU code”; JVM turns it into native code as needed.


🔹 Key Responsibilities of JVM
- Load .class files (ClassLoader)
- Verify bytecode (security / validity)
- Execute (Interpreter + JIT)
- Manage memory (Heap, Stack, GC / Metaspace)
- Threads, synchronization, exceptions


🧠 JVM Architecture (Components)

| Component                  | Description                                      |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| **Class Loader**           | Loads .class into memory                         |
| **Bytecode Verifier**      | Checks safety / validity before run              |
| **Runtime Data Areas**     | Heap, Stack, Method Area/Metaspace, PC, …        |
| **Execution Engine**       | Interpreter + JIT (+ GC as part of runtime)      |
| **Garbage Collector**      | Reclaims unreachable Heap objects                |
| **JNI (Native Interface)** | API to call / be called from C/C++ native code   |


📦 JVM Memory Areas

| Area                    | Description                                              | Thread?   |
| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- | --------- |
| **Heap**                | Objects / arrays                                         | Shared    |
| **Stack**               | Frames: local vars, operand stack, partial results       | Per thread|
| **Method Area**         | Class metadata, static vars, constant pool, method info  | Shared    |
| **PC Register**         | Address of current bytecode instruction                  | Per thread|
| **Native Method Stack** | Frames for native (non-Java) methods                     | Per thread|

Clarifications (interview traps):
- Method Area ≠ “all methods’ code only”. It holds class-level data:
  class/method/field metadata, runtime constant pool, statics, annotations info, etc.
- HotSpot (Java 8+): Method Area is implemented mainly as **Metaspace** (native memory), not the old PermGen.
- Native Method Stack ≠ JNI.
  JNI = interface/API to native code.
  Native Method Stack = memory area used while a native method runs.


Examples of bytecode instructions:

| Bytecode         | Meaning                             |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `iload_0`        | Load int from local slot 0          |
| `iconst_1`       | Push int constant 1                 |
| `iadd`           | Add two ints                        |
| `invokevirtual`  | Call instance method (virtual)      |
| `ireturn`        | Return int from method              |


🔸 Example

public int sum(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

Bytecode (instance method → slot 0 is `this`, a=1, b=2):

0: iload_1
1: iload_2
2: iadd
3: ireturn

→ load a, load b, add, return int


🔍 Where Are These Instructions?
- Stored inside .class files
- JVM reads & executes them
- View with:  javap -c MyClass


✅ Bytecode summary
- Defined by the JVM Specification
- Platform-independent
- Generated by javac, executed by a platform-specific JVM


🔁 JVM Execution Flow

.java
  ↓ javac
.class (bytecode)
  ↓
JVM
  ClassLoader → Verifier → Execution Engine → output
                         ↘ Interpreter
                         ↘ JIT (hot methods → native code)


🆚 JVM vs JRE vs JDK

| Feature      | JVM              | JRE                | JDK                      |
| ------------ | ---------------- | ------------------ | ------------------------ |
| Purpose      | Execute bytecode | Run Java apps      | Develop + Run            |
| Contains     | Execution engine | JVM + libraries    | JRE + compiler + tools   |
| Can Compile? | ❌                | ❌                  | ✅                        |


🧠 Interview Tips
- JVM is **platform-dependent** (Windows JVM ≠ Linux JVM)
- Bytecode is **platform-independent** → “write once, run anywhere”
- JIT improves performance by compiling hot bytecode → native code
- Security: verifier + class loader sandboxing model


🔄 Types of Class Loaders

1) Bootstrap ClassLoader
   - Loads core classes (java.lang.*, java.util.*, …)
   - Native (C/C++), parent of all
   - Modern JDK: from the modular runtime image (not old jre/lib story)

2) Extension → Platform ClassLoader (Java 9+)
   - Old: lib/ext (Extension ClassLoader)
   - Java 9+: Platform ClassLoader → platform modules (java.sql, java.desktop, …)

3) System / Application ClassLoader
   - Loads from classpath (-cp / CLASSPATH / module path app classes)
   - Child of Platform ClassLoader
   - Loads most of YOUR application classes

4) Custom ClassLoader
   - Extend java.lang.ClassLoader
   - Use cases: encrypted/remote classes, hot reload (Tomcat, OSGi, Spring DevTools)

Parent Delegation (short):
  Child asks Parent first → only loads itself if parent didn’t find the class.
  (Full hierarchy → see "Types of Class Loaders" notes)


🧠 Interview one-liner
“JVM executes platform-independent bytecode using class loading, verification, interpreter/JIT, and managed memory (Heap + Metaspace + per-thread stacks).”
